// @check
// @error member access '.v' on non-object S?
// CONTRACT (must-error): A PROPERTY-PATH GUARD DOES NOT NARROW A LOOP BODY, and that is a
// decision rather than an oversight — the gate the loop-body narrowing is shipped with.
//
// A bare name has no aliases: a local is the only spelling of its slot, so a guard's fact
// about it can only be falsified by a write the narrowed scope already rejects. A PATH
// (`o.v`) is different — the fact is keyed by a path string over an object any number of
// other names can reach, and nothing re-points the WRITE side of that overlay at the
// declaration. Measured in the `if` spelling, which does narrow paths: with a
// `clr(o) { o.v = null }` in the arm, `if o.v != null { clr(o); o.v.length }` is
// `vl check`-clean and TRAPS, and the `boolean` rep prints a stale value instead. Carrying
// that into loop bodies would have added those cells to the silent classes, so the loop
// keeps rejecting a path guard's body — the verdict it already had.
//
// The bare-name spelling of the very same read works (`FIELD-S` in
// `while-body-narrowing.vl`), so this file pins the BOUNDARY, not a missing feature.
type O = {v: S | null}
type S = {v: i32}
function f(o: O) {
  while o.v != null {
    return o.v.v
  }
  return 0
}
print(toString(f({v: null})))
